\par\par \b \uc2  China Copper -  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  bet buoys prices. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nailene Chou Wiest \par \uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 12:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 7 (Reuters) - Speculation that China's yuan might succumb to the pressure of other falling Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed China's domestic copper prices this week, analysts and traders said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  While three-month copper prices on the London Metal Exchange dropped to $1,637 on Thursday from the $1,700 level last Friday, Shanghai copper futures netted a small gain to 16,580 yuan ($1,997) at Friday's close, up from 16,500 yuan a week ago. \par \par \uc2  "Half of the speculative money in the market was betting on the yuan's devaluation," said Chen Jinhua, senior analyst at Jinyuan Futures. \par \par \uc2  China is a net importer of copper, which is priced in dollars, and a yuan devaluation would boost copper's value in yuan terms. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that the Shanghai copper futures rose in four out of five sessions this week, ostensibly in response to "better fundamentals" of China's domestic copper market. \par \par \uc2  "Players like to say output cuts and an anti-smuggling campaign could reduce a copper surplus even if demand isn't picking up," a trader said. "But many players are actually making a bet on the yuan." \par \par \uc2  Last month six major copper producers pledged to cut output by 90,000 tonnes and 15 small producers also said they expected their production to fall by 70,000 tonnes this year. \par \par \uc2  Keeping its word, the Daye Nonferrous Metals Company idled its 120,000-tonne smelter on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that speculative activities in copper picked up this week almost in tandem with rising worries over the yuan despite the Chinese government's repeated pledge to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in one week to defend the yuan at the key level of 8.28 to the dollar as pressure from the falling Japanese yen went unabated. \par \par \uc2  Copper producers, however, were still selling futures and making deliveries to the exchange warehouses, traders noted. \par \par \uc2  "Producers are happy to sell at the highs and that keeps a lid on the futures prices," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  Chen at Jinyuan said that while speculators were taking substantial positions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , producers were not. \par \par \uc2  Producers had cash-flow problems, which constrained them from taking large speculative positions, he said. \par \par \uc2  For example, two medium-size copper producers owed at least two months of back wages, and a speedy way for them to get cash was to use warehouse warrants as collateral, he said. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai Metal Exchange copper stocks surged to a record high this week at 77,838 tonnes, up 2,501 tonnes on the week. \par \par \uc2  A yuan depreciation would boost the value of the existing stocks, but it would also make concentrate imports more expensive, Chen said. \par \par \uc2  "Producers don't have much to gain from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8702edk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
