\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan opens down vs dollar on yen, HK rates. \b0\par\par\uc2 279 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 04:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 7 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan opened down against the U.S. dollar in Shanghai on Friday due to the weakness in the Japanese yen and Hong Kong's higher local interbank rates, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan opened at 8.2799 to one dollar against Thursday's close of 8.2795 on the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, the tightly controlled national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Despite early losses, dealers said they expected the yuan to find firm support at the 8.2800 level because of expected support for the local unit from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to buy the yuan for the second time in a week. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has exerted psychological influence on the market," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Bank traders believe sellers will be deterred from pushing the local unit below the 8.2800 level," he said. \par \par \uc2  China has vowed repeatedly not to devalue the yuan, but the market worries weakness in the Japanese yen could force Beijing to lower the value of the local unit in order to protect its export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's continued weakness and high Hong Kong interbank rates triggered fresh selling in the yuan," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The yen stayed at 144.38/43 to one dollar in early trade in Tokyo against 144.22/32 in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong interbank rates rose at the open on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ($1=8.3 yuan) \par \par \uc2  - Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  - Email: reutersh@uninet.com.cn. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du870269k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
