\par\par \b \uc2  OUTLOOK-Reuters Asia Business at 0730 GMT. \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1998\par 09:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Following are some of the main business news stories (all times GMT) \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  FINANCIAL MARKETS \par \par \uc2  TOKYO/LONDON - The rapid rise in dollar versus the yen since Japan named its new cabinet prompts fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Tokyo's Nikkei 224 index falls 0.88 percent, which combined with Wall Street's one percent decline looks set to weigh on European stocks. German stocks are trading more than 0.5 percent adrift in early electronic trade. \par \par \uc2  ** Markets wraps from Asia and Europe \par \par \uc2  From TOKYO \par \par \uc2  ** Separate story on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \par \par \uc2  ** Analysis on renewed market speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be forced to devalue if the yen's fall continues \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  JAPAN/ECONOMY \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japan's beleaguered new government goes on the offensive to protect the sagging yen, warning global financial markets to beware of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ** Spot coverage of meetings between U.S. Ambassador Thomas Foley and Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi (0840), Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa (0900), Obuchi and business group leaders (0840), Miyazawa and ruling party tax panel members (0600) \par \par \uc2  ** Updated focus wraps \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WEATHER/ASIA \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - East Asia in the grip of its worst floods in decades as killer-rains claims thousands of lives in China and South Korea, compounding the region's economic misery. \par \par \uc2  ** Wrap of extreme weather conditions around Asia, including forecasts for heavy rains to hit the Pacific and reports of limited disruption to farm, mining and industrial production \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KOHL/INTERVIEW \par \par \uc2  ST GILGEN, AUSTRIA - German Chancellor Helmut Kohl gives pre-election interview from his holiday home to RTL television. First extracts from interview around 1130. \par \par \uc2  ** Spot cover and focus \par \par \uc2  BONN - Finance Minister Theo Waigelnews conference on German government's long-term finance policy (0830). \par \par \uc2  ** Spot cover \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  U.N. INSPECTIONS CHIEF LEAVING IRAQ \par \par \uc2  BAGHDAD - Chief U.N. weapons inspector Richard Butler is cutting short his visit to Baghdad after encountering serious difficulties in talks with Iraqi leaders. \par \par \uc2  ** Updated focus \par \par \uc2  ** Oil markets reaction \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  If you have any queries, please call: \par \par \uc2  Singapore (65) 870-3815 \par \par \uc2  Comments to: asia.newseds@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8400swg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
