\par\par \b \uc2  China's Li Peng urges Tokyo to revive economy. \b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1998\par 19:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Aug 3 (Reuters) - China's parliament chief Li Peng has urged Japan's Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi and his new cabinet to ease pressure on China by invigorating the Japanese economy, the official Xinhua news agency said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "China's economy is confronted with some problems due to the pressure of the Southeast Asian financial crisis, and China needs an improving exterior environment while depending on its own efforts to tackle the difficulties," Xinhua quoted Li as telling a visiting Japanese business executive. \par \par \uc2  "China hopes that Japan, as China's largest trade partner, will initiate effective financial and economic policies to resurrect its economy, since doing so would help ease and overcome the crisis," said Li, chairman of the National People's Congress. \par \par \uc2  Li made the remarks in a meeting with Tsutomu Kanai, president of Hitachi Ltd, in the coastal resort of Beidaihe on Monday, Xinhua said. \par \par \uc2  Last week, China welcomed the election of former foreign minister Obuchi to the premiership but issued firm reminders of the high regional stakes resting on Japanese economic policy. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong pointed out that Japan had an "obligation and responsibility to maintain financial and economic stability in Asia", state media have said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's recent decline had "increased the pressure on economic restructuring of some countries and regions in East Asia" and had "a certain impact on China's exports and use of foreign capital", state media have quoted Dai as saying. \par \par \uc2  The yen continued to grind weaker on Monday, trading at 145.76 to the U.S. dollar from about 141 one week ago. \par \par \uc2  China has persistently said it cannot tolerate a sharply weaker yen, calling on Japan to demonstrate leadership by dealing swiftly with its economic problems. \par \par \uc2  Li's comments underscore Beijing's sense of alarm at policy drift in Japan, which takes in 17 percent of China's exports and accounts for 10 percent of its foreign direct investment. \par \par \uc2  As Asia struggles to get back on its feet, China faces slowing exports and lower-than-expected economic growth, which threaten to undermine the pace of vital economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Dai's warning on the weaker yen last month spearheaded \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's successful efforts to spur U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the flagging yen. \par \par \uc2  The central banker and other officials hinted that a weaker Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes it difficult for Beijing to stick to its policy of keeping the Chinese yuan firm. \par \par \uc2  ($1.0 = 8.3 yuan) \par \par \uc2  - Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-6532-4978 \par \par \uc2  - Email: beijing.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du83021gm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
