\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan stocks up, yen down, Asia mixed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Martin \par \uc2 851 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 13:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 31 (Reuters) - Japan stocks ended the week with a bounce on Friday on hopes the new government would take bold steps to repair the economy, but markets elsewhere in Asia were mixed as they shifted focus from Japan to domestic concerns. \par \par \uc2  The yen, however, dropped sharply after new Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he intended to let the market dictate the value of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks. \par \par \uc2  "Put simply, the yen and stocks should be left to the markets," Miyazawa told a news conference early in the day. \par \par \uc2  He said the government could step in in an "extreme situation" such as the recent joint U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step the yen's steep slide, but added: "the markets are intelligent, they can't easily be fooled". \par \par \uc2  After losing ground in Asia, the yen was at 144.15 to the dollar by 1000 GMT in Europe, compared with 143.68/73 in late Thursday trade in New York. \par \par \uc2  The yen bearishness was compounded by the announcement that the June jobless rate had hit a record 4.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise stalled briefly earlier in the day in Tokyo after Miyazawa tried to water down the comments, calling them a "common sense view", but the damage was already done. \par \par \uc2  "Although he (Miyazawa) apparently tried to erase the impact of his overnight comments in the morning, what he said overnight could be a real intention," said a bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The new government's expected moves had the opposite effect on share prices, however, as traders bought on hopes that the cabinet of new Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi would be forced to take drastic measures to shock the country's economy out of its seven-year funk. \par \par \uc2  "There are two issues: sorting out the financial crisis and getting the economy started again," said Celia Farnon, manager at Nomura Securities. "They've got to deal with these ASAP." \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei 225 index rose 177.37 points or 1.09 percent to 16,378.97. September Nikkei futures rose 190 to 16,360. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa, a veteran politician seen as an improvement over recent finance ministers, has said he prefers tax cuts to fiscal stimulus to prod consumer spending. Obuchi has called for six trillion yen in tax cuts, as well as a 10 trillion yen extra budget. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Malaysian shares closed higher, with the key Composite Index rising 3.37 percent on a technical rebound, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the Composite Index was up 13.12 points to 402.65. Dealers said prices were expected to rise after a sharp selldown on Wednesday, when the index ended at a nine-year low for the third day running. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares managed small gains, buoyed by a 1.26 percent rise on Wall Street overnight. But investors were unwilling to do much ahead of heavyweight HSBC Holdings Plc's results on Monday, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index ended 30.04 points, or 0.38 percent, higher at 7,936.20 points. \par \par \uc2  Australia's All Ordinaries index also followed Wall Street higher, rising to a morning high of 2,735.2. But nearly all those gains were lost after institutions dumped resources group the Broken Hill Pty Co Ltd, and the index ended barely firmer at 2,704.6, a mere 3.3 points higher. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said some institutions were caught with BHP stock following Thursday's expiry of July-dated options. \par \par \uc2  Stock prices in Taipei dropped one percent as chronic worries over Taiwan's economy and an unclear second half outlook pushed the index lower, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The main index ended 76.92 points lower at 7,653.51 \par \par \uc2  South Korean stocks closed slightly lower on Friday after fears of a devaluation of the Chinese yuan pared earlier gains which had been prompted by ample liquidity, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Stock Exchange composite index closed 1.58 points lower at 343.33. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday to support the yuan, buying at the 8.2800 level. The yuan closed at 8.2799 against the U.S. dollar, little changed from Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the yen has sparked fears China may be forced to devalue the yuan, a dark prospect for other Asian economies that compete against Chinese exports. \par \par \uc2  The Jakarta stock index ended slightly lower as some major blue chips retreated on profit-taking after early gains, dealers and analysts said. The composite index closed down 0.97 points or 0.2 percent at 481.72 points. \par \par \uc2  Stocks in Bangkok closed barely changed at 266.72 as investors awaited the start next week of the International Monetary Fund review of Thailand's progress in fixing its ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  Philippines shares dipped 5.89 points to 1607.61. \par \par \uc2  Singapore stocks finished firmer after a flurry of late afternoon activity seen as month-end buying, dealers said. The benchmark Straits Times Industrials Index ended up 4.67 points at 1064.20 at the close. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du7v013jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
