\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS-Yuan seen holding, pressure mounts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gill Tudor \par \uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 18:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 31 (Reuters) - China is widely expected to defend its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against mounting pressure from a tottering yen, but some analysts doubt it will be able to fight off the challenge in the medium term. \par \par \uc2  As the yen slipped again on Friday, breaching 144 to the dollar on laissez-faire \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments by Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to pull the yuan back from the key level of 8.28 to the U.S. unit. It closed at 8.2799. \par \par \uc2  "We still think they won't devalue, because there's no absolute necessity for them to do so," said Rupert Norman, a China analyst at M&G Investment Management in London. "They certainly have enough fire-power to keep off the speculators." \par \par \uc2  But Graham Neilson, Asia regional economist at Banque Paribas in London, was more bearish. \par \par \uc2  "It seems inevitable they will have to devalue, probably in the next 12 months," he said. "They will be under enormous pressure to take the political decision to devalue." \par \par \uc2  Neilson said he expected the yuan to move to between 9.50 and 10 to the dollar by this time next year, based on the assumption of a yen rate between 160 and 180 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  China has repeatedly said it is determined not devalue the yuan, also known as the renminbi, despite the havoc wreaked on other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  But the weaker yen is weighing heavily on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only because of the generally bearish sentiment it generates in Asia but because Beijing is looking to Japanese imports to lift its faltering export growth. \par \par \uc2  "They will have to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pressure's not going to go away," said Neilson. \par \par \uc2  London analysts said the People's Bank of China had ample \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - estimated at around $140 billion - to combat continuing pressure on the yuan, even though considerable capital outflows were exiting the country unofficially through the back door. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts argue that China should logically come under less pressure from a weaker yen than many other emerging Asian economies because, unlike some of them, it is not in direct competition with Japan in other export markets. \par \par \uc2  "Some of the pressure is unfounded. Competitively, Japan and China are not really related - their manufactures are not comparable," said one London analyst who asked not to be named. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the yuan is not fully convertible. Despite its black market, China's capital controls do give the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  some protection from speculative shifts in "hot" money. \par \par \uc2  And while continued market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pose some problems, analysts say devaluation would solve nothing for China. \par \par \uc2  External trade accounts for a relatively small proportion of China's gross domestic product (GDP), estimated at around 23 percent, and many of its exports have a high import content. \par \par \uc2  More importantly, the analysts say, devaluation of the yuan would spark a further round of devaluations across Asia - including pressure to crank down the Hong Kong dollar peg against the U.S. dollar - which would leave China back at square one in terms of regional competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  M&G's Norman said he expected the government to concentrate on the domestic economy in coming months, trying to seal off loopholes in capital controls with a view to cutting interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "As the economy deflates there should be less pressure on the yuan anyway," he said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to hold the line into the medium term, although there might be some downward adjustment in 18 months' to two years' time. \par \par \uc2  But Paribas's Neilson said China's slowing GDP growth could tip into recession over the next year, and mounting economic woes could force the government to bite the bullet on devaluation. \par \par \uc2  China's economic growth slowed to an official seven percent year-on-year in the first half of 1998 - the lowest rate seen since 1991 - but many independent analysts put annual GDP growth closer to three or four percent. ((London Newsroom +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du7v0363q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
