\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  - Regionals struggle as yen sinks. \b0\par\par\uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0900 GMT - The Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained soft as the yen flopped through 144 per dollar in early European trading. \par \par \uc2  The yen was hurt by comments from new Japanese finance minister Kiichi Miyazawa, who suggested a policy of benign neglect may be followed toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said: "Put simply, the yen and stocks should be left to the markets" except for "an extreme situation" like the recent Japan-U.S. joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi pledged to fix Japan's economy for the sake of the nation, Asia and the world but conceded he was no "superman". \par \par \uc2  Earlier Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, repeating a long-standing U.S. plea, said that Japan needed to act promptly to resolve problems in its banking sector and to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  *** The won bucked the regional trend firming on month-end exporter dollar sales with the market shrugging off the yen's renewed weakness. \par \par \uc2  Buying by overseas investors wanting to purchase the host of Korean assets that are on sale at the moment also helped the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said the worst was over for South Korea although further reforms were still needed. \par \par \uc2  "The haemorrhaging has been stanched... I believe we have seen very important progress," Summers told reporters at an event organized by the U.S. Overseas Private Investment Corp trade agency. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "There is very hard work to do, particularly in the financial sector and in carrying out structural reform." \par \par \uc2  But further reforms are likely to stir up the existing labour problems in the country with working strongly opposing moves to lay-off both white and blue collar workers. *** The baht was also a little easier with the general trend. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand said it expects 1998 GDP to contract by between 6.0-7.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  In its monthly data release the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported a current account surplus for May of $1.01 billion, a trade surplus in May of $949 million and a June Balance of Payments deficit of $808. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reported total net forward positions of $12.2 billion at July 24 when foreign reserves stood at $27.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  *** The rupiah was finding some support just below the 13,000 per dollar on news extra finaicial help was on the way. \par \par \uc2  International donors on Wednesday pledged $7.9 billion in aid to Indonesia, to be disbursed during the 1998/99 fiscal year, which ends in March 1999. \par \par \uc2  The World Bank said the amount, together with an exceptional International Monetary Fund package of more than $6 billion announced on July 16, will cover the 1998/99 Indonesian budget deficit, estimated at 8.5 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  More than 30 donor countries and organisations were included in the pledges and they welcomed the Indonesian government's commitment to boost the economy. \par \par \uc2  *** The ringgit was a little softer with traders looking to the background political situation. \par \par \uc2  The market first pricked up its political ears after Daim Zainuddin was appointed Minister of Special Functions with responsibility for the economy. \par \par \uc2  This was seen driving a wedge between Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad and his deputy Anwar Ibrahim. Anwar is also finance minister. \par \par \uc2  The latest development comes from a Malaysian cabinet minister who on Thursday told political parties in the ruling coalition to prepare for snap general elections to ensure their mandate in tackling the country's economic woes. \par \par \uc2  Information Minister Mohamed Rahmat did not say when the general elections will be held, but said they could be called at any time, the national Bernama news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  From a technical point of view the ringgit, having failed to break the 4.10 level earlier this week, should move to the weaker end of its 4.10 to 4.16 range. \par \par \uc2  *** The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the yuan at the key 8.2800 level, helping the local unit close at 8.2799 per dollar, little changed from Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan faced fresh pressure on Friday due to renewed falls in the yen," one dealer said. "This triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local unit." \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the yen has sparked fears China may be forced to devalue the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du7v0354f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
