\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China intervenes \highlight\uc2  to defend yuan as yen sags. \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 10:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Bill Savadove \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 31 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has come under pressure by a sagging Japanese yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold dollars in a thin market with the yuan trading at the key 8.2800 level. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan faced fresh pressure on Friday due to renewed falls in the yen," one dealer said. "This triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local unit." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly vowed not to devalue the yuan despite the withering effect of the Asian financial crisis on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the region. \par \par \uc2  But Beijing has looked warily at the weakness of the yen, which it believes is complicating its campaign to hold the value of the yuan intact. \par \par \uc2  "The thinking of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not changed," said another dealer. "They want to defend the yuan, but the pressure is clearly there. Many companies tend to hold U.S. dollars now, so the supply in the market has decreased." \par \par \uc2  China has seen its export growth slow this year, largely due to weak purchasing power around Asia, and it is hoping that a stronger yen will boost Chinese exports to Japan. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Beijing is also angered that Japan does not appear to be shouldering its fair share of the burden in helping Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  regain stability. \par \par \uc2  The United States and Japan jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on world \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend the yen last month after Beijing hinted loudly that it might reconsider its pledge not to devalue if the yen sank much lower. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, contacted in Beijing, said he did not know of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "I am not aware of, and have not received any notice of, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," the official said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a frequent participant in the tightly controlled local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was clearly aimed at holding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above the 8.28 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The effort succeeded as the yuan closed at 8.2799 to one dollar, little changed from Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The yen came under pressure again after Japan's new Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said the yen and domestic stock prices were best left to the market, a policy the market dubbed "benign neglect". \par \par \uc2  The pressure on the yen eased by the afternoon as the dollar stood at 143.60/70 yen in late Asian trade from a high of 143.85 earlier and 143.68/73 in late New York trading on Thursday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed a number of other recent signals that it was stepping up its campaign to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong was quoted as saying that China would strike hard at black market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the week, Beijing announced tough penalties on banks making improper \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements. \par \par \uc2  - Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  - Email: reutersh@uninet.com.cn. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du7v034mr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
