\par\par \b \uc2  China says Japan has strength to stop yen fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 254 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1998\par 02:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, July 30 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong has said Japan has the "strength" to prevent the yen from depreciating further, the Financial News reported on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The depreciation of the Japanese yen has its economic reasons, but Japan as one of the world's economic powers has the strength to stop the yen from depreciating further," the newspaper quoted Dai as saying at a meeting of the Communist Party on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Japan has an "obligation and responsibility to maintain financial and economic stability in Asia", Dai said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's fall has "increased the pressure on economic restructuring of some countries and regions in East Asia", Dai said. \par \par \uc2  It also has had "a certain impact on China's exports and use of foreign capital", Dai said. \par \par \uc2  Beset by slowing exports and economic growth, China has expressed alarm about Japan's weak economy. Beijing last week voiced hope that Keizo Obuchi, who is expected to be appointed Japan's next prime minister, can revive Japan as a key engine of regional growth. \par \par \uc2  Despite pressures for a competitive devaluation, China has repeatedly pledged not to devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Last month \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the flagging yen by dropping hints a weaker Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would force it to reconsider its policy of keeping the Chinese yuan firm. \par \par \uc2  - Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-6532-4978 \par \par \uc2  - Email: beijing.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2