\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Lower; Trade Still Stable Under PBOC's Watch \b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1998\par 09:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan fell a touch against the U.S. dollar Friday in steady dealings, as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its watch over the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when needed to hold the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in balance. \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand for dollars was heightened slightly by a faltering Japanese yen as the country chooses a new prime minister. But they added that tight trading regulations in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market meant the demand didn't translate into significantly increased dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar ended Friday at 8.2799 yuan (CNY), up from CNY8.2794. It traded between CNY8.2779 and CNY8.2799. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible under the current account only, or for trade in goods and services. That leaves the unit virtually immune from speculative attack. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of China, continued to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Friday, entering the market to buy yuan as commercial banks increased their dollar purchases a touch to match demand from exporting firms, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Exporters - who, under a new regulation, are allowed to retain up to 15% of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earned - are increasing their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings on fears that a weak yen could force Beijing to lower the yuan. \par \par \uc2  However, demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is capped by the 15% regulation, and traders said high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows into China and a strong balance of payments will continue to bolster the yuan in coming months. \par \par \uc2  A cheaper yen would make Chinese exports to Japan more expensive and cut into Japanese investment to China. Japan is one of China's main export markets and a major source of foreign direct investment. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  24-07-98 \par \par \uc2  0723GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914du7o00nhu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
