\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Higher; Trade Still Watched By  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1998\par 09:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan (CNY) closed a touch firmer against the U.S. dollar Wednesday in range-bound dealings monitored by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sold some dollars to hold the semi-convertible yuan steady, market observers said. \par \par \uc2  They added that news of a slight fall in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - which could indicate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is propping up the yuan - didn't affect trade. \par \par \uc2  An official at the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves shrunk to between $140.5 billion and $140.6 billion at the end of June. They were at $140.91 billion through May. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar ended Wednesday at a day low of CNY8.2795, down from Tuesday's CNY8.2799. It traded between CNY8.2795 and CNY8.2799 per dollar, with most transactions concentrated around CNY8.2796, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  Market observers played down dollar-selling on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the amount was likely small. "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is significant," said Joe Zhang, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with HSBC James Capel Asia in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is tradable under the current account only, meaning that capital flows are tightly restricted. \par \par \uc2  Participants said commercial bank dollar purchases remained relatively strong Wednesday as investors watched the Japanese yen slip slightly lower ahead of the selection of a new Japanese prime minister Friday. They added, though, that the increase in demand for dollars was slim. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen would make China's exports to Japan and foreign investment from there more expensive. Japan is one of China's largest export markets, as well as a major investor in the mainland. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  22-07-98 \par \par \uc2  0758GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914du7m00prv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
