\par\par \b \uc2  China's Foreign Reserves/End-June -3: Selling Dlrs In Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1998\par 12:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Despite the decline in reserves, China's foreign trade surplus reached $22.6 billion at the June. That was $4.8 billion more than same month in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Shaky confidence in the yuan - caused by regional and domestic worries - may be causing companies to sit on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than convert into yuan. Under a new rule, Chinese exporters are allowed to retain 15% of their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings. \par \par \uc2  Government economists have blamed the flat \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  growth on the Asian crisis, saying it has undermined confidence in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The most negative impact the Asia crisis has had on China isn't its effects on exports or foreign investment, but rather its effect on domestic confidence in the yuan exchange rate," the State Development Planning Commission's Macroeconomic Research Institute said in a recent report. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible under the current account only, or for trade in goods and services, which shields it from speculative attacks. But regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations coupled with China's own slowing economy and halting reforms have raised concerns that it might choose to devalue the yuan to spur export growth. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials have pledged not to devalue the yuan this year and its exchange rate to the dollar remains rock-solid in domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar ended Wednesday at a day low of CNY8.2795, down from Tuesday's CNY8.2799. It traded between CNY8.2795 and CNY8.2799 per dollar, with most transactions concentrated around CNY8.2796, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  However, the session saw the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling dollars for yuan, although market observers played down both the amount involved and the market pressure on the local unit. "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is significant," said Joe Zhang, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with HSBC James Capel Asia in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 22-07-98 \par \par \uc2  1029GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du7m067ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
