\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  PBoC Gov/Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -2: In Meeting With Rubin \b0\par\par\uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 04:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING (Dow Jones)-- \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Dai Xianglong Friday welcomed recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and Japan to bolster the yen, telling U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin that Chinese authorities were "very happy" with the joint action. \par \par \uc2  Dai, speaking to Rubin during a photo session ahead of a private meeting at the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that China appreciated Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers' visit last week to Tokyo in which he underscored the importance of Japan undertaking broad-based reforms to help bolster the yen. \par \par \uc2  Besides noting that Chinese officials also had good discussions with Summers at a meeting of finance deputies from the Asia Pacific and Europe, Dai remarked to Rubin: "We were very happy with the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and Japan to help support the yen." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. and Japan reportedly sold upwards of $4 billion earlier this month in a concerted effort to bolster the sagging Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came at a time when there was mounting speculation in financial markets that a weakening yen might prompt China to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, for the first time in four years and set off a chain reaction of competitive devaluations throughout Asia. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Rubin, who is holding a series of meetings Friday with top Chinese economic and monetary officials, didn't immediately react to Dai's remarks and reporters were asked to exit the private meeting room. \par \par \uc2  Joining Rubin was U.S. National Economic Council Chairman Gene Sperling and Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs David Lipton. \par \par \uc2  The only remark by Rubin was in jest when the Chinese central banker made some brief opening remarks and said he hoped to hear some good news from the top economic policy-maker. \par \par \uc2  "Well, it'll bring us useful conversation," Rubin said of his meeting with Dai. "I don't know about good news or not." \par \par \uc2  -By William Murray; bmurry@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0257GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6q05gb4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
