\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends A Bit Higher; Steady Ahead Of Clinton Visit \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1998\par 07:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan strengthened slightly against the U.S. dollar Thursday, as the semi-convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bucked weak sentiment in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to close virtually unchanged for another day. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was again active in the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady. Concern over a sliding Japanese yen has led to increased dollar buying in recent sessions, but traders say the PBoC is determined to hold the yuan steady, particularly during U.S. President Clinton's visit to China this week and next. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar ended Thursday at 8.2796 yuan (CNY), down from Wednesday's CNY8.2798. It traded in a range of CNY8.2796 and CNY8.2801. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tradable under the current account only, or for trade in goods and services. The government has pledged not to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite sliding export growth as a result of devaluations in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a particularly close watch over the market in the next two weeks during Clinton's visit. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, in other trading Thursday, the Hong Kong dollar closed at CNY1.0692, down from Wednesday's CNY1.0693, while the yen fell to CNY5.8757 per 100 yen from the previous close of CNY5.9021. \par \par \uc2  In the non-deliverable forward market, which gives an indication of how much participants are willing to pay for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a specific maturity in the future, money broker Exco Group in Singapore on Thursday was quoting the U.S. dollar at CNY8.7596 for delivery in six months and at CNY9.2796 over a one-year timeframe. The six-month rate now carries a premium of CNY0.48 over the spot rate, compared with CNY0.50 the previous session, while the one-year rate held a premium of CNY1.00, unchanged from Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The high premiums and implied high interest rates suggest that many banks believe the yuan may depreciate in the months ahead, despite government pledges to hold the unit steady. \par \par \uc2  -By Karby Leggett; (8621)6218-3268; kleggett@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  25-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0543GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914du6p00mb9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
