\par\par \b \uc2  Taiwan Ctrl Bk/Reserves -5: Open To Joint Effort With China \b0\par\par\uc2 215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1998\par 13:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  "The accusations started from Hong Kong," he said. "They said if Taiwan didn't devalue, then Hong Kong wouldn't be facing speculative attacks. They wanted Taiwan to continue defending the exchange rate but they weren't paying the cost." \par \par \uc2  For political reasons, Taiwan hasn't discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy with Hong Kong or the mainland, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is open to the idea of a Greater \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan. \par \par \uc2  "We all have good economic fundamentals, abundant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," Shea said. "If we discussed about cooperation, we could talk about sharing the cost of defending local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  A Greater \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort, or even the threat of one, could have "prevented speculative attacks" on the Hong Kong and New Taiwan dollar, Shea said. \par \par \uc2  Regardless, there's little chance the Hong Kong dollar or the Chinese yuan will devalue, especially given the increasing stability in the yen after the U.S. and Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is confident "Japan should find some way to solve their problems," Shea said. "The U.S. won't let the yen devalue too much." \par \par \uc2  -By Betty W. Liu; (8862) 2502-2557; bwliu@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-06-98 \par \par