\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong stocks rocket higher at opening on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 04:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, June 18 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks soared at the opening on Thursday after U.S. and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight to prop up the weak yen brought relief to markets around the region. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng blue chip index opened on a gain of 6.01 percent at 8,485 points. The red chip Hang Seng China-Affiliated Corporations Index jumped 8.11 percent to 965 and the H-share index added 6.09 percent to 458 points. \par \par \uc2  The June futures contract on the Hang Seng Index rocketed to 9,000 points at the opening, a rise of 1,010 points from Wednesday's close. It later pared those gains but was still up 510 points at 8,500. \par \par \uc2  "Right now there is a turnaround in all world markets. This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been regarded very favourably," said Howard Gorges, a director at South China Brokerage. \par \par \uc2  "Some stocks are up 10 to 20 percent and that is mostly due to short-covering," he said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve and the Bank of Japan jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight selling U.S. dollars for Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar, which rose to an eight year high of above 146 yen earlier in the week, plunged to 136 yen in New York and fell to 135.60 yen in early Asian trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "This is a positive signal to the markets and a hopeful sign for economies in Asia that the U.S. has started to get involved in Japan and that Japan might get on track which would be helpful for the rest of Asia," said Gorges. \par \par \uc2  "The worry recently has been that yen weakness would affect other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including potentially the renminbi. If the renminbi devalued it might bring the Hong Kong dollar down with it. Now people are more relaxed about the stability of the renminbi." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor welcomed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen and said it was unnecessary to devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Fears of a yuan depreciation had hit Hong Kong's China-related stocks hard and the sector roared back on Thursday. New World Infrastructure Ltd  led the gainers, soaring HK$2.75, or 34.16 percent, to HK$10.80. \par \par \uc2  The stock had been hit over recent sessions due to concerns about its U.S. dollar debt and renminbi earnings. \par \par \uc2  "China plays will outperform the other stocks as they had been seriously oversold," said Raymond Yin, a director at Core Pacific Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  Among the blue chips, Cheung Kong Infrastructure Holdings Ltd  jumped HK$2.30, or 17.36 percent, to HK$15.55 and Hutchison Whampoa Ltd gained HK$4.00, or 11.70 percent, to HK$38.20. \par \par \uc2  A drop in local interbank rates spurred the market on. The overnight rate was quoted at 7.00-8.00 percent compared with 7.00-10.00 percent at Wednesday's close and the three-month rate at 8.00-10.00 percent compared with 8.50-11.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The market is crazy," said Eugene Law, a director at Lippo Secuities. "People are just ignoring the price and running to cover short positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Law said the Hang Seng Index had overshot on the upside in early trade and should stabilise at around 8,500 points. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip index later extended its early gains to trade up 600 points, or 7.50 percent, at 8,604. Turnover was HK$3.44 billion after the first 45 minutes of trade. \par \par \uc2  "We have had speculative forces turning a difficult situation into a very bad sitation in the last month and now these speculative forces have to unwind so that is helpful," said Gorges. \par \par \uc2  "But the fundamentals in Hong Kong are going to remain difficult. Today, if the market holds a gain of five or six percent, then I think it will be doing pretty well." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said after the initial jump, investors would sit back and watch for developments in Japan. U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was linked to Japanese economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  "At some point people will start wondering what the Japanese will actually announce," said Gorges. \par \par \uc2  - Jennifer Genevieve (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02r1v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
