\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Jochimsen says Japan must act to avert crash. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Crossland \par \uc2 564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 14:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DUESSELDORF, Germany, June 18 (Reuters) - Bundesbank council member Reimut Jochimsen warned on Thursday that world markets could face a "big crash" if Japan fails to implement fundamental domestic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Jochimsen said it was vital that China and Hong Kong avoided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations, but that they would not be able to do so unless Japan can convince financial markets it is serious about reviving its economy. \par \par \uc2  "We must avoid a big crash," Jochimsen told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  He said a lack of market confidence in Japan's efforts to reform its financial system was the principal reason for the recent weakness of the yen, which on Monday fell to an eight-year low of 146.55 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen's drop has put pressure on China and Hong Kong to devalue in response which analysts say could spark competitive devaluations across Asia, deepening the region's crisis and heightening the risk of global market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  Jochimsen said Wednesday's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and Japan to buy yen could pay off only if accompanied by credible reforms. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't replace reform," Jochimsen said. \par \par \uc2  The yen on Thursday held on to gains made overnight in New York trading after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which lifted the yen to about 137 against the U.S. dollar from about 142. The yen stood at 136.60 at 1130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "It is of central importance that China maintains (the level of) its two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... as well as the Hong Kong dollar. They won't be able to do that if Japan merely tackles its foreign trade business," Jochimsen said. \par \par \uc2  Short-term stimulation packages were not sufficient but needed to be backed by longer-term policy change, he said. \par \par \uc2  The impact of the Asian crisis on the world economy was likely to last longer than had been assumed previously, Jochimsen said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had eased the pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hong Kong to devalue. \par \par \uc2  But they added that the outlook for the yen remained completely uncertain and depended in part on the outcome of a crisis meeting in Tokyo starting Thursday between U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers and senior Japanese officials. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government pledged on Thursday to move aggressively on economic and financial reform, disposing of bad loans through measures such as asset securitisation and taking steps to boost domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Jochimsen said growing competition from east and southeast Asian competitors in the coming months was likely to continue limiting the scope for price increases for industrial products and raw materials. \par \par \uc2  "For us as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is good news," Jochimsen said. \par \par \uc2  He added that Germany's gross domestic product was likely to grow at a faster pace in 1998 than the 2.2 percent growth experienced in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Jochimsen, regarded as an interest rate "dove" on the Bundesbank's council, also said that domestic demand had significantly increased since the beginning of the year in Germany, fuelling a recovery that had previously been mainly export-driven. \par \par \uc2  Jochimsen is the president of the state \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of North Rhine-Westphalia, which released a report showing that sentiment in many industries in Germany's most populous state had reached its highest level since May 1993. ((Frankfurt Newsroom +49 69 756525, frankfurt.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02li0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
