\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  PBoC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  In Yuan Mkt With New Trade Limits \b0\par\par\uc2 265 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 13:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--Fearing instability in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leapt to support the yuan Monday by introducing even-tighter restrictions on market movements and then buying and selling dollars in line with demand, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and officials at the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The stepped-up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets worldwide continue their close watch over the Japanese yen, which fell through the Y145.00-per-dollar point Monday for the first time since August 1990. Many economists and analysts believe a weak Japanese yen will put even greater pressure on China to let the yuan slide, despite repeated government pledges to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, traders said the yuan remained rock-solid. But growing anxiety over the future course of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was evident from the wider-than-usual spreads that emerged at the start of trade. And that prompted the PBoC to take action, the participants said. \par \par \uc2  "We put our buy orders in for the dollar and they were all rejected," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a state bank. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the rates and that was where orders were transacted." \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed Monday at CNY8.2798, down just slightly from CNY8.2799. It traded between CNY8.2798 and CNY8.2799 through the session, an extremely tight range even for China's semiconvertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which can be bought and sold for trade-related transactions only. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 15-06-98 \par \par \uc2  1135GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6f053ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
