\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends marginally higher against dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 05:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 15 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan ended Monday marginally higher against the U.S. dollar in Shanghai as sentiment to the local unit was helped by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support last week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  It closed at 8.2798 to one dollar against 8.2799 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There was more dollar selling today after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local unit last week," a dealer said. Dealers did not see \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice last week, buying the yuan to prevent it from falling below the key 8.2800 support level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Editorials in China's leading financial newpapers on Monday lashed out at Japan and the United States for failing to halt the yen's slide, saying the two were pursuing their own interests at the expense of beleaguered Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  Despite China's determination to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said the Chinese yuan was still under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Pressure for the yuan to go down is still heavy mainly because the Japanese yen continues to fall on the world markets," a second dealer said. "We cannot exclude the possibility that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market again when it wants to support the yuan." \par \par \uc2  Most dollar selling on Monday came from domestic banks in a slow market, making it easier for the yuan to close above the 8.2800 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan moved in a narrow range for the whole session between 8.2798 and 8.2799 and dealers said the yuan was likely to move around the 8.2800 level in the next few days. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan is unlikely to fall sharply in the short term as Beijing is not willing to see it devalue at least for the time being," a third dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Trading was dull with most business done at 8.2798. \par \par \uc2  "Why are Japan and the United States letting the yuan slide," asked the Financial News, published jointly by China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and leading state banks and financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  "Mainly because a falling yen suits the two countries' economic interests now and is beneficial in passing on their domestic problems to other countries," the daily said in its frontpage commentary on Monday. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the yuan ended up against Japanese yen at 5.6936 to 100 yen from 5.7561. It ended down against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0707 to one dollar from 1.0696. \par \par \uc2      - Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2      - Email: reutersh@uninet.com.cn \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests\tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab     .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6f0336b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
