\par\par \b \uc2  China's  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  reserves plateau. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Browne \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 14:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, June 12 (Reuters) - China announced on Friday its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit $140.91 billion at the end of May, but its torrid pace of increase levelled off amid Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meanwhile sold dollars on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the Chinese yuan from falling because of bearish trade data and a tumbling Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  China has the world's second largest trove of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves after Japan as a result of swelling trade surpluses and huge foreign investment inflows. \par \par \uc2  Over the past several years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought large volumes of dollars to keep the yuan from appreciating, adding to the reserves. \par \par \uc2  But the Asian crisis has cut the growth of China's exports. Investment from the region, the most important source of overseas finance, also has dried up. \par \par \uc2  In 1997, China's reserves surged by $34.9 billion to reach $139.9 billion at the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  According to Friday's figures, they grew by just $1.01 billion in the first five months of this year. At end-March the reserves were at $140.6 billion, earlier figures showed. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Shanghai said the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second time this week to hold up the yuan. \par \par \uc2  The bank would continue to support the yuan at above the key 8.2800 level, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought whenever the yuan approached the key 8.2800 level," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The bank has been defending the 8.2800 level but \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were rare in the past few months, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the yuan for what dealers said was the first time in months after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a six-month closing low of 8.2805 last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Shanghai-based market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 China Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, does not post trading volumes. \par \par \uc2  The Chinese yuan ended Friday at 8.2799 against Thursday's 8.2796. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan was still under the pressure of China's slowing exports," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Dai Xianglong, warned on Tuesday a sliding yen was harming Chinese exports and foreign capital inflows. He urged Tokyo to take action to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dai's call was repeated on Thursday by a Chinese government spokesman, who urged Japan to show "courage and wisdom" to halt the yen's slide. \par \par \uc2  But the spokesman insisted China's policy of not devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  China recorded a trade surplus of $18.53 billion in the January-May period. But export growth of 8.6 percent during the period compared with 20.9 percent growth for the whole of last year. \par \par \uc2  Worryingly, exports declined in the month of May for the first time in 22 months. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen hit a nearly eight-year low of 144.75 to the dollar in Tokyo on Friday morning after U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the only cure for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness was restoring economic growth in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in New York said the Bank of China had been a sporadic buyer of Japanese yen against the dollar in recent days and weeks. \par \par \uc2  Bank of China had also been a frequent seller of U.S. treasury bills, fuelling speculation that the bank may be using some dollar reserves to support the yen, the dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of China official in New York said the bank was not involved in any such dealings. \par \par \uc2  - Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-6532-4978 \par \par \uc2  - Email: beijing.newsroomzreuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6c038a8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
