\par\par \b \uc2  China stocks mostly up at midday on stamp duty cut. \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 07:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 12 (Reuters) - China's stock markets were mostly higher on Friday at midday after the government cut stamp duty on stock transactions. \par \par \uc2  State television said on Thursday after the markets closed that China would cut the stock stamp duty for both buyers and sellers to 0.4 percent from 0.5 percent from Friday, in a move which effectively trimmed trading costs. \par \par \uc2  "The financial effect is virtually zero but the effect on sentiment after months of being starved for news is good," said a broker at a foreign securities firm in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  Only Shanghai's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B share index fell as worries over weakness in the Japanese yen and a fall on Wall Street overnight sparked selling, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's B share index was down 1.072 points, or 2.53 percent, to 41.281 on turnover of $1.25 million against $1.47 million on Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  But Shenzhen B shares gained 0.48 point, or 0.57 percent, to 84.37 on turnover of HK$6.0 million ($769,231) compared with HK$3.9 million on Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's domestic A share index gained 26.451 points, or 1.85 percent, to 1,456.107. In Shenzhen, the A share index climbed 11.45 points, or 2.58 percent, to 455.85. \par \par \uc2  Despite the optimism, brokers ruled out further sharp gains due to worries over the Asian financial crisis, including weakness in the Japanese yen and falls in regional markets. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, amid fears the plunging Japanese yen may force Beijing to devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  B share investors were also holding their breath to see if the cut in stamp duty was part of a long-awaited package to boost the market, rumoured to include the approval of Sino-foreign joint venture mutual funds, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "The B share market's future still relies on whether the state will adopt exclusive measures to help B shares," said an analyst at Everbright Securities in Shenzhen. \par \par \uc2  ($1.0=8.3 yuan) ($1.0=HK$7.8) \par \par \uc2  - Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  - Email: reutersh@uninet.com.cn. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6c02x16\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
