\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Dn As  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Slips Past Key Support Level \b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 1998\par 08:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan (CNY) ended narrowly lower against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, in thin trade that saw the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slip past a key support level - CNY8.2800 per dollar - for the first time in 1998, despite balanced demand for the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed at CNY8.2805, up from CNY8.2797. It traded between CNY8.2850 and CNY8.2795. The CNY8.2850 is the lowest point the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reached in 1998. \par \par \uc2  Traders said it wasn't clear why the yuan fell through the psychological barrier given that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be well-supported by strong capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were aware of news reports citing Chinese Foreign Ministry officials as saying they couldn't rule out resumption of nuclear tests in the wake of similar tests by India and Pakistan. But they said the reports didn't impact on trade. "It seems like a trader might have made a mistake and imputed a wrong figure," said one dealer on the exchange floor. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible under the current account only, or for trade in goods and services. That has left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  immune from the type of large-scale capital movements that have rocked regional markets over the past year. In fact, many large-scale transactions on the yuan exchange are prearranged by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which keeps a close watch on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in trade to prevent sharp fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  Backing up the controls, the government has pledged not to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China's new premier, Zhu Rongji, has made that vow repeatedly. Market participants in China widely view the promises as credible, and note that if the government were to backtrack on it now, it would risk a crisis of confidence in both China and Hong Kong, which holds the last true U.S.-dollar pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  03-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0628GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914du6300koe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
