\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Up On Light Yuan Buying Before Close \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 1998\par 08:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended higher against the U.S. dollar Thursday, rising late in the session on limited yuan buying, possibly by the China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market traders said. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed at CNY8.2790 from CNY8.2795, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had remained over the past three days. It traded between CNY8.2790 and CNY8.2798 during the session, with most transactions taking place around CNY8.2795. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only convertible for trade in goods and services, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on a regular basis to ensure that the yuan remains within strict trading limits to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Despite the yuan's strong fundamentals, based on high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows and growing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently come under selling pressure as trading companies buy dollars to hedge against an anticipated depreciation. They say China must lower the value of the yuan in order to keep exports competitive. \par \par \uc2  But China's government has repeatedly denied the yuan will be devalued. Economists cite the need for cheap imports and maintaining the value of domestic savings as reasons not to devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  In other trading Thursday, the Hong Kong dollar closed at CNY1.0688, up from Wednesday's CNY1.0680 while the yen ended at CNY6.0917 per Y100, up from the previous close of CNY6.0659. \par \par \uc2  In the non-deliverable forward market, which gives an indication of how participants expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade in the coming few months, money broker Exco Group in Singapore on Friday was quoting the U.S. dollar at CNY8.5500 for delivery in six months and at CNY8.9600 over a one-year timeframe. \par \par \uc2  There is an ongoing belief by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders that China will sometime be forced to reduce the value of the yuan, market traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As well as being only semi-convertible, the yuan is controlled by\par tight trading margins, both with official price limits for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and a strong trading presence by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stabilize even\par slight fluctuations.\par -By Catherine McKinley; (8621)6279-7294; cmckinley@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 21-05-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0609GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5l04bu9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
