\par\par \b \uc2  China PBoC/Yuan Mkt -2: Stability Ahead Of Clinton Visit \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1998\par 14:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Traders said the PBoC jumped into the market at the start of trade Monday. Dealings were thin through the first half of the session, but expanded near the close as participants realized orders were being transacted only at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -mandated level. \par \par \uc2  "Some banks decided to wait, but others had to fulfill orders, so they had no choice," said another dealer, adding that some banks were waiting for a stronger dollar before buying. \par \par \uc2  Another trader attributed the tighter PBoC trading limits to U.S. President Bill Clinton's visit to China later this month. "There is no chance at all that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move sharply in either direction before Clinton leaves," said another dealer. "It's too sensitive." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't new. The PBoC has been an active trader in the market since it was established in late 1994. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's activity Monday underscores growing concern that even slight dips in the yuan are making already-nervous \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets even more anxious. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed Monday the tighter trading limits. "We are providing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market stability," said an official at the PBoC. He added, however, that the new limits are part of efforts to combat nervous markets. Once the uneasy sentiment abates, he said he expects the yuan to see more upward pressure from still-strong capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  Despite slowing export growth, China posted a trade surplus of $18.53 billion through the first five months of the year. Traders say the positive capital inflows are still underpinning strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Other traders noted that loopholes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market have led to the heightened watch over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier in the year, commercial businesses run by China's military, the People's Liberation Army, were actively buying dollars, thinking they could benefit from a devaluation, sources at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  say. That's because the military here previously had the right to buy dollars anytime it wanted. Set decades ago, that provision was revoked following a nationwide investigation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the first quarter, the PBoC sources say. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 15-05-98 \par \par \uc2  1202GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5f044zz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
