\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends At Historic High As Dollar Buying Slows \b0\par\par\uc2 270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1998\par 08:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended up against the U.S. dollar Thursday, hitting an historic high as commercial banks slowed their dollar buying and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yuan to climb, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan reached 8.2779 yuan per U.S. dollar - its highest level since the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center opened to trade in March 1994, the exchange confirmed. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed at CNY8.2779 from CNY8.2782 on Wednesday. It traded between CNY8.2777 and CNY8.2781, with most transactions taking place at around CNY8.2781. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is tradable under the current account only. \par \par \uc2  Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows into China, and a government policy which requires exporters to convert most of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into yuan pressures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate. That pressure is countered by People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, which holds the yuan stable. \par \par \uc2  However, recent fears of a yuan devaluation - in order to keep Chinese exports competitive against those from Southeast Asian countries - led to heavy dollar buying by commercial banks for trading companies wanting to hedge against a more expensive dollar. \par \par \uc2  That dollar buying pulled China's yuan even lower and reinforced fears the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could fall further, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But the release of first quarter economic figures earlier this month - showing export growth still strong at 12.8% - has improved investor confidence that China won't be forced into competitive devaluation, they added. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 30-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0647GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4u03mk6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
