\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Higher On Last-Minute Local  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Buying \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1998\par 08:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended a touch higher against the U.S. dollar Friday after losing through the morning on commercial bank dollar buying, but rising before the close on heavy yuan buying, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed at CNY8.2788, down from Thursday's CNY8.2794. It traded in a range CNY8.2788 and CNY8.2796 with most transactions around CNY8.2795. \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks bought dollars Friday to cover demand from trading companies seeking to cover import costs, traders said. Others bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a hedge against a weaker yuan, they added. \par \par \uc2  'People are still worried that the yuan may be devalued in order to keep China's exports competitive against other Asian exports,' said one trader. \par \par \uc2  The government has repeatedly pledged not to devalue China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but some market observers say a gradual yuan depreciation is likely as the country's economy slows and exports slip. Exports are considered one of China's main engines for growth and few expect the government to allow them to slide, particularly as official economists already expect China to miss its 8% growth target in the first half of this year. \par \par \uc2  Worries over a strengthening dollar have lead to increased \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, analyst said. \par \par \uc2  'The People's Bank of China has exercised patience as strong dollar buying has bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to almost CNY8.2800. But it entered the market Friday to drag the dollar down a few minutes before the close of trade,' said one trader with a commercial bank. \par \par \uc2  According to a second trader, the dollar will rebound next week as demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains strong, 'but the yuan won't (be allowed to) fall below CNY8.2800.' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  10-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0613GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010917du4a004cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
