\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Slightly Higher On Technical Rebound \b0\par\par\uc2 249 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 1998\par 08:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan closed a touch higher against the U.S. dollar Monday, as a technical rebound following declines last week pushed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, trader said. \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed at 8.2796 yuan (CNY), down from the previous close of CNY8.2798. It traded in a range of CNY8.2795 and CNY8.2798, though most transactions were done close to the day's high, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the technical correction is unlikely to last through the week, however, as dollar demand from importers remains strong while yuan demand has slipped on a slowdown in exports, traders said. \par \par \uc2  'With the Japanese yen weak against the dollar, there's growing demand to import Japanese goods,' a trader with a state commercial bank said. \par \par \uc2  Increased dollar demand has brought on recurring market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has sold dollars to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a narrow trading range, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is convertible under the current account only, or for trade in goods and services, and isn't vulnerable to the same market forces that sparked a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in Asia last year. \par \par \uc2  Newly appointed Premier Zhu Rongji and other Chinese leaders have pledged to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value, despite sharp regional devaluations, which are making China's exporters less competitive. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 06-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0606GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4602w0b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
