\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Down Vs Dlr But Mkt Still Seen Stable \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 06:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan slipped a touch against the U.S. dollar Monday, as foreign-trade companies bought dollars. But traders said the yuan continued to trade in an exceptionally tight range, and that strong \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows are still pointing to stability in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center, the dollar closed at CNY8.2790, up only slightly from Friday's CNY8.2789. The dollar moved in a range of CNY8.2787 and CNY8.2793 during the session. \par \par \uc2  The yuan's slide Monday was its second straight. Traders underscored, however, that the consecutive declines don't indicate a new trend for the yuan, which is still convertible under the current account only, or for trade-related reasons. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is monitoring market movements closely, and actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slips out of its current band around CNY8.2800. That is particularly true as China's National People's Congress meets in Beijing to hammer out details on key state enterprise and government reforms. The congress meets through the end of this week. \par \par \uc2  Congress aside, traders also noted that all signs indicate the central government will hold steady on its pledge not to devalue - or even depreciate - the yuan this year. The vow, reiterated again over the weekend by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top official, Dai Xianglong, is aimed in part at providing stability to regional financial markets. That's because a yuan devaluation now is seen by many as setting off another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations and further pushing back a timetable for an Asian economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 09-03-98 \par \par \uc2  0546GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du39020xo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
