\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan to survive Asia woes for now-US experts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Lerner \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Dec 30 (Reuters) - The flurry of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations across Asia might eventually force China to revise its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but Beijing will likely stand by current policy over the next year, U.S. analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Chinese financial authorities have steadfastly vowed to defend the yuan, as well as the Hong Kong dollar, and, given its massive stockpile of international reserves and current account surplus, Beijing has the means to do so. \par \par \uc2  But financial markets are watching to see how long China, with its reliance on export-led growth, can pursue its staunch defense of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when its Asian competitors -- Korea, Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand -- have allowed their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to devalue by 30-50 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It is a background risk that we think is possible particularly if down the line signs of China's export growth start to peeter out," said Devi Aurora, Asia analyst at DRI/McGraw Hill, a research firm that surveys global risk. \par \par \uc2  "It would take two to three years of signs that export growth is evaporating before the Chinese start to think about what they can do to revive their export sector," Aurora said. \par \par \uc2  Beijing's massive trade surplus, coupled with the country's $140 billion stockpile of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, should hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up, but a decline in capital inflows would add to the case for devaluation, Aurora said. \par \par \uc2  Pledged foreign investment -- most aimed at the export sector -- in the first 10 months was down 28 percent to $48.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  China may find the sweeping Asian devaluations a tough pill to swallow, but giving in to the pressure for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could undermine the stability of Hong Kong and confidence in China's economic emergence. \par \par \uc2  "China has a lot of reasons to stay where they are at least for the time being," said one analyst at a major U.S. hedge fund. " I don't think they would be forced to devalue -- they have capital controls, a current account surplus." \par \par \uc2  The capital controls and limited amount of foreign debt has shielded Beijing from the pressures that dragged down economies across Asia. \par \par \uc2  "Because of fairly strong growth rates and fairly strong, although diminished, flows of investment, the Chinese probably have a little more room than some of the more pessimistic (market speculation)," said David Rothkopf, managing director at Kissinger Associates. \par \par \uc2  In fact, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the surging value of yuan resulting from a large trade surplus. On Tuesday, the yuan was at 8.2795 to the dollar, hovering close to a 30-month high. \par \par \uc2  Market fears were that a yuan devaluation could trigger a debt crisis that would force Hong Kong to abandon the stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime that pegs the local dollar to the U.S. unit at HK$7.8 to US$1. \par \par \uc2  China has not yet felt the bite of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  collapse. China registered a trade surplus of $40.23 billion in the first 11 months of 1997, a huge surge over the $13.93 billion in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  Economic growth has slowed from the torrid 9.5 percent pace in the first half of this year, but is still forecast at 8.8 percent in 1997, far outpacing its ailing Asian neighbors. \par \par \uc2  But the elevated growth rates so critical to China are predicated on the country's ability to export, and exports are precisely the sector hit hardest by the Asian devaluations, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Ron Leven, global markets strategist at JP Morgan in New York, said China would have to see a dramatic loss of competitiveness over the next year and a half before feeling the need to take strong action on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If you started to see their current account surplus dropping sharply over the course of this year, particularly if it was because of competitiveness with its neighbors, or just because of global slowdown, then it becomes a more real possibility," Leven said. \par \par \uc2  Rothkopf said he considered the most likely scenario for China would be a gradual devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a few months, after the financial markets got over the anxiety of six months of steep Asian devaluations. \par \par \uc2  (( Emerging Markets Unit, 212-859-1671)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcu00kdj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
